 ## Running
 
+Whole stack (LocalStack + API + dashboard) in one command:
+
+```bash
+task run-local
+```
+
+That runs `setup-db`, starts the API detached, then holds the terminal with
+the dashboard. `task stop-local` brings the containers back down — scoped to
+this worktree, so it leaves other worktrees' stacks alone.
+
+Dashboard on its own, when the API is already up:
+
 ```bash
-pnpm --filter @attribution-service/dev-dashboard start
+task run-dashboard
 ```
 
 The app boots at <http://localhost:5174>. The dev server binds only to
 the loopback interface, so it is not reachable from other machines on
 your network.
 
+Prefer these tasks over `pnpm --filter … start`. They source
+`scripts-ext/worktree-config.sh`, which exports `VITE_API_BASE_URL` for this
+worktree's API port. Started directly, the dashboard falls back to `:3024` and
+silently talks to whichever worktree owns the default port — or nothing.
+
 ### Talking to local
 
-Start the API in another terminal:
+Select **Local** in the environment picker. `/readyz` will respond
+without an API key. Everything else needs a key from `API_KEYS` in
+`.secrets.env`.
 
-```bash
-task run-service
-# API on http://localhost:3024
-```
+`.secrets.env` is gitignored, so a fresh worktree does not have one — copy it
+from the main checkout before `task run-local`.
 
-Select **Local** in the environment picker. `/readyz` will respond
-without an API key.
+In a worktree the API port is computed from the worktree path (3200-3299), not
+3024; `task setup-db` and `task run-local` print the resolved ports.
 
